ATTRIBUTION / THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL

SCOPE. The MIT licence above applies to the CODE in this repository and to
nothing else. Dictionary text is never MIT. Specifically:

  MIT (code)
    lsgloss.py, clean_gloss.py, ground.py, suspect.py, qa_report.py,
    score.py, consensus.py, refine.py, verify.py, eval/run_eval.py,
    eval/xref.py, Makefile, requirements.txt, and the prose of README.md

  CC BY-SA 4.0 (derived from the dictionary -- see section 1 below)
    eval/gold.tsv          25 reference glosses written from Lewis & Short
    overrides.tsv          gloss corrections quoted from Lewis & Short
    out/*.tsv              all generated output, including scores, consensus,
                           and the corpus frequency list (see 1c)
    the example glosses quoted in README.md

  prompts/*.txt are original instructions written for this project (MIT), but
  they quote a handful of dictionary words as examples.

Anything not listed as MIT above is derivative of the Perseus text and inherits
CC BY-SA 4.0. The model weights and the inference engine are covered by their
own licences (sections 2 and 3); none of these third-party materials are
redistributed here -- each is fetched by the setup steps in README.md.

1. THE DICTIONARY  (source of all glosses in this project)

   Charlton T. Lewis and Charles Short,
   "A Latin Dictionary: Founded on Andrews' edition of Freund's Latin dictionary"
   Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879.

   Machine-readable text prepared by the Perseus Project, Tufts University,
   under the direction of Gregory Crane, with funding from the National
   Endowment for the Humanities. Copyright Trustees of Tufts University,
   Medford, MA. First released 1997-10-28.

   File used: CTS_XML_TEI/perseus/pdllex/lat/ls/lat.ls.perseus-eng2.xml
   Source:    https://github.com/PerseusDL/lexica
   Licence:   Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International
              https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/

   CHANGES MADE TO THE LICENSED MATERIAL (required by CC BY-SA section 3.a.1.B):
   entry text was programmatically condensed into short English glosses of
   roughly one to five words using a language model; cross-reference entries
   were resolved to the gloss of their target entry; bibliographic citations
   were removed from the resulting glosses. No dictionary text is reproduced in
   full. The glosses are a derivative work of the Perseus text.

   BECAUSE CC BY-SA IS COPYLEFT, the generated glosses are NOT covered by the
   MIT licence above. Anyone redistributing them must credit Lewis, Short, and
   the Perseus Digital Library as set out here, state that changes were made,
   and license the result under CC BY-SA 4.0 International.

1b. THE TREEBANKS  (source of gold form/lemma pairs in morphology.csv)

   Perseus Ancient Greek and Latin Dependency Treebanks
   Source:  https://github.com/PerseusDL/treebank_data
   Licence: Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike
            https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/us/

   The Latin treebanks supply human-verified form/lemma pairs, used at the
   highest confidence in morphology.csv. They generalise beyond the annotated
   works -- "cui" is a form of qui in any text -- so they improve lookup for the
   untreebanked works this project exists to serve.

   Being CC BY-SA, they carry the same copyleft as the dictionary text, so the
   generated package remains CC BY-SA 4.0 and must credit Perseus for both.

1c. THE CORPUS  (source of out/latin_freq.tsv)

   Perseus canonical-latinLit
   Source:  https://github.com/PerseusDL/canonical-latinLit
   Licence: Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0 United States
            https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/us/

   428 Latin editions, ~6.87M running tokens. Used only to count how often each
   word form occurs; no corpus text is reproduced here or in the package. The
   resulting frequency list (out/latin_freq.tsv, built by corpusfreq.py) decides
   which sense a reader is shown for an ambiguous form, and weights every
   quality figure in the README by how often a word is actually met.

   A word-frequency table is a set of facts about a text rather than an
   expressive excerpt of it, but the corpus is CC BY-SA and the derived list is
   distributed here under the same terms regardless, alongside the same
   attribution as the dictionary and treebanks.

2. THE MODELS

   Three open-weight models are used, in sequential passes. None is
   redistributed here; each is downloaded by the setup steps in README.md.

   a. openai/gpt-oss-20b                          -- glossing
      https://huggingface.co/openai/gpt-oss-20b
      Apache License 2.0
      Weights are used as published (MXFP4); this project converts them to
      GGUF locally with llama.cpp's converter, which repacks rather than
      requantises. No modified weights are distributed.

   b. google/gemma-4-31B-it-qat-q4_0-gguf         -- independent judging
      https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-4-31B-it-qat-q4_0-gguf
      The model repository carries an `apache-2.0` tag, but its license_link
      points to Google's Gemma-specific terms:
          https://ai.google.dev/gemma/docs/gemma_4_license
      These are not the same licence. Anyone redistributing this pipeline's
      output, or using it commercially, should read Google's Gemma terms
      directly rather than relying on the repository tag -- the Gemma terms
      carry a prohibited-use policy that Apache 2.0 does not.

      Note on scope: Gemma is used here only to SCORE candidate glosses, i.e.
      to emit a digit 0-5. It does not author any gloss text in the released
      dataset. Rows it caused to be repaired were rewritten by gpt-oss.

   c. meta-models/Muse-Glimmer-30B-GGUF           -- hard-case adjudication
      https://huggingface.co/meta-models/Muse-Glimmer-30B-GGUF
      Apache License 2.0
      First-party GGUF published by Meta; no third-party requantisation.

3. THE INFERENCE ENGINE

